2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] I can help you understand Tor and .onion services safely.
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] • Getting Started*
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] Download the Tor Browser from torproject.org — this is the standard way to access .onion sites.
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] • Finding Links*
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] Rather than sharing specific URLs (which change frequently and carry security risks), look for:
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] - Ahmia.fi or OnionSearch — search engines that index legitimate onion services
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] - r/onions on Reddit — community-curated lists (verify before visiting)
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] • Legitimate Examples*
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] Many mainstream services maintain onion mirrors for privacy:
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] - DuckDuckGo search
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] - ProPublica (journalism)
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] - Facebook
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] - The New York Times
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] • Critical Warnings*
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] - Many onion sites host illegal content (drugs, weapons, CSAM). Accessing these carries legal
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] consequences regardless of anonymity.
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] - Never download files or enable scripts unless you understand the risks.
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] - Tor hides traffic from your ISP, but entry/exit nodes can be monitored.
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] If you're looking for specific content (privacy tools, journalism, whistleblowing platforms), I can
2026-01-31 00:45:16 [kimi-k2.5] point you toward legitimate resources. What are you trying to access?
